      "text": "{\"unit_id\": \"Hrere\", \"choices\": [\"C2\"], \"reasoning\": \"C2 Ḥrr.t ('Hereret') is the single feminine word ḥrr.t, 'flower'. Record 1 Hrere renders ḥ-r-r with added vowels and drops the feminine ending t, which is allowed. That is exactly this word, the standard rendering of the name of the wife of Piankh, so it matches. C1 Ḥrwr ('Herwer') is one word ḥ-r-w-r. If w is left out, the consonants fit. But Herwer is a different word, a place-name type form, not ḥrr.t 'flower'. Record 1 read as a modern spelling renders the flower word, so C1 is a different name. C3 Ḥr.w-wr ('Hor-wer') is two words, 'Horus' + 'great'. The consonants can be forced to fit, but Hrere does not render 'Horus-the-great'; it renders the single word ḥrr.t. So C3 is a different name.\"}"
